Allocate two buffers each with N records of supplied width W bytes, plus M metadata bytes. Find total bytes. Width includes all padding; stack, heap allocator overhead and other objects are excluded. Supplied values: {"m":16,"n":40,"w":6}

Result: 496 bytes. Two arrays consume 2*N*W bytes and metadata consumes M. Compare this subtotal with an exact target's available memory only after accounting for all other allocations.

Authored reference project: double-buffer memory plan. Components are ideal mathematical elements with supplied values; no unspecified manufacturer rating is inferred.

A supplied unsigned 8-bit payload needs an even-parity bit. Return 0 or 1 so payload plus parity contains an even number of ones. Supplied values: {"payload":173}

Result: 1 bit. The parity bit is the payload population count modulo two; adding it makes the count even.

Authored reference project: telemetry parity encoder. Components are ideal mathematical elements with supplied values; no unspecified manufacturer rating is inferred.

An ideal I2C write sends one address byte and N payload bytes at the supplied clock rate Hz. Count nine clocks per byte including ACK. Find clocked time in seconds; exclude START/STOP timing, arbitration and stretching. Supplied values: {"hz":100000,"n":2}

Result: 0.00027 s. Clock demand is 9*(1+N), divided by the supplied clock frequency. This is not an end-to-end transaction latency guarantee.

Authored reference project: register transaction budget. Components are ideal mathematical elements with supplied values; no unspecified manufacturer rating is inferred.

Use the explicitly defined ideal unsigned FLOOR-bin quantizer code=floor(Vin*2^N/Vref). N is bits; voltages are V; 0<=Vin<Vref. Return integer code. This mathematical model is not a physical ADC transfer-function claim. Supplied values: {"bits":10,"vin":1.1,"vref":3.3}

Result: 341 code. The model has 2^N equal-width bins. Select the bin whose lower boundary includes Vin and whose upper boundary excludes it; physical ADC offset/gain and endpoint conventions need its datasheet.

Authored reference project: quantizer reference model. Components are ideal mathematical elements with supplied values; no unspecified manufacturer rating is inferred.

For a hypothetical linear regulator with input/output V and load current A, calculate dissipated W. Ignore quiescent current. Values are supplied operating assumptions, not approved component limits. Supplied values: {"i":0.12,"vin":9,"vout":5}

Result: 0.48 W. Input power minus load power gives heat: (Vin-Vout)*I. Junction temperature still requires the exact part, thermal path and ambient conditions.

Authored reference project: bench supply heat estimate. Components are ideal mathematical elements with supplied values; no unspecified manufacturer rating is inferred.

For a supplied dimensionless error and positive gain, compute demand clipped to [0,1]. This is a static proportional controller without integral or derivative action. Supplied values: {"error":3,"gain":0.2}

Result: 0.6 fraction. Multiply gain by error; project the result onto the actuator interval [0,1]. This calculation alone makes no stability claim.

Authored reference project: bounded actuator demand. Components are ideal mathematical elements with supplied values; no unspecified manufacturer rating is inferred.

Supplied task execution budgets C1,C2 and periods T1,T2 are in ms. Find total processor utilization as a fraction. This is a load estimate, not a schedulability or deadline proof. Supplied values: {"c1":2,"c2":3,"t1":10,"t2":20}

Result: 0.35 fraction. Average demand is C1/T1+C2/T2. Interrupt overhead and blocking are excluded from these supplied budgets.

Authored reference project: periodic task capacity. Components are ideal mathematical elements with supplied values; no unspecified manufacturer rating is inferred.

Two unloaded resistor branches share a supplied excitation V. Each branch has top and bottom resistors in ohm. Calculate left midpoint minus right midpoint voltage. Supplied values: {"lb":1100,"lt":1000,"rb":1000,"rt":1000,"v":5}

Result: 0.119047619048 V. Each midpoint follows its bottom resistor fraction of excitation; subtract the right voltage from the left. This ideal unloaded bridge model excludes sensor tolerances.

Authored reference project: balanced sensor bridge. Components are ideal mathematical elements with supplied values; no unspecified manufacturer rating is inferred.

Add two supplied signed 16-bit integer samples with saturation to [-32768,32767]. Return the stored integer code; wraparound is forbidden. Supplied values: {"a":30000,"b":10000}

Result: 32767 code. Add in a wider accumulator, then clamp between the signed 16-bit limits before storage.

Authored reference project: audio accumulator limiter. Components are ideal mathematical elements with supplied values; no unspecified manufacturer rating is inferred.

Send N bytes with one start bit, D data bits, P parity bits and S stop bits per frame at the supplied baud. Find ideal wire time in seconds, excluding idle gaps. Supplied values: {"baud":9600,"d":8,"n":40,"p":0,"s":1}

Result: 0.0416666666667 s. There are N*(1+D+P+S) serial bits; divide by baud in bits/s. Buffering latency is outside this ideal wire-time calculation.

Authored reference project: serial capture buffer. Components are ideal mathematical elements with supplied values; no unspecified manufacturer rating is inferred.

N independent zero-mean samples each have supplied RMS noise sigma in V. Find RMS noise of their arithmetic mean. Noise is uncorrelated and stationary; correlated drift is excluded. Supplied values: {"n":16,"sigma":0.008}

Result: 0.002 V-rms. Variances add for independent samples. Division by N in the mean scales variance by N squared, leaving sigma/sqrt(N) RMS noise.

